Modified by Republic contributors for a Python 3.11+ single-call SDK:
removed agent, hook, replay, UI, telemetry, tool execution and client persistence;
replaced generic request/executor machinery with one Provider protocol; retained
text/file/reasoning/tool data and block-based event aggregation; added strict
event ordering, detached snapshots, recursive metadata merging, unknown usage,
explicit response identity and deterministic stream cleanup. Files use tagged
URL/base64 strings. Tool arguments remain raw strings, including invalid JSON.
Tests retain relevant aggregation, metadata, missing-terminal and early-exit
scenarios and add interleaving and cancellation coverage. This is a modified
subset, not a claim of upstream API or behavior compatibility.

Step 2 extracts only Chat Completions conversion and streaming semantics. It
adds native non-streaming generation, owned/borrowed client lifetime, disabled
SDK retries, input validation, pending tool-header buffering, required provider
finish evidence, tail usage and HTTP/SSE fixtures. History repair, implicit file
downloads, provider/model routing and OAuth are not included. Responses is
added separately in Step 3.

Step 3 adapts Responses conversion and selected event semantics. Native
non-streaming generation, full-history store=False requests, raw output-item
persistence, strict delta/snapshot reconciliation, encrypted-only reasoning,
explicit terminal status mapping and deterministic resource cleanup are Republic
changes. Item IDs and call IDs stay distinct. Shared client lifetime/error code
is extracted into src/republic/providers/_openai_client.py. Hosted tools,
server-side conversation state, media operations and upstream history repair are
excluded; no upstream agent/executor machinery is retained.

Step 4 adapts the Anthropic Messages conversion and selected stream semantics.
It adds native non-streaming generation, owned/borrowed client management without
SDK retries, redacted thinking, complete signature aggregation, explicit cache
control and inclusive input usage (uncached + cache reads + cache writes). Missing
counters remain unknown; cumulative stream usage replaces earlier counters.
Raw argument fragments are never parsed/repaired during streaming. Leading system
blocks preserve order; later system messages are rejected rather than hoisted.
Unlike upstream, no role merging, history repair, model-specific default token
limit, hosted tools or execution/agent code is retained.

